 | FG-002e | P1 | TODO | Sweep the interrupt/deadline surface for remaining early-return paths | Reviews 9-12 of PR #14 each found ONE more code path that returns before polling the abort predicate: archive mid-loop, archive after the final copy, archive zero-match, junit mid-loop, junit zero-match. The pattern is "every early return is a missed poll", and it was found one instance per round rather than swept. **Raised to P1 and widened after PR #15**: the same pattern recurred in brand-new code four more times (`stash` copy loop, `stash` predicate missing the deadline, `unstash` restore, `deleteDir` recursive delete), and a sibling pattern appeared alongside it — an interrupted step reporting `Aborted` when a failFast *sibling* was the cause, which is the collateral-outranks-cause bug already fixed twice elsewhere. Acceptance: enumerate every return point in `Publish`, `Executor` and the walker; assert each either polls or provably cannot be reached after an interrupt; and assert every abort classifies its CAUSE (deadline → aborted, sibling → failure) rather than assuming one |
 | FG-010b | P0 | **DONE** | Zero-argument step calls silently swallowed the NEXT step | Found by the `stash-unstash` receipt: `before.txt` was missing from Fogell's workspace and nothing else was wrong. `deleteDir()` consumed `()`, then looked for inline arguments starting with `ws` — which crossed the NEWLINE and ate the entire following step as a positional argument, which was then DISCARDED because parenthesised args take precedence. So the build skipped a step and reported success. Affected every zero-arg call form (`deleteDir()`, `cleanWs()`, …). A step is now either `name(args)` or `name args`, never both, and the inline form must begin on the same line |
 | FG-044b | P1 | TODO | Four findings landed on PR #15 review 7, after the merge | Ticketed rather than lost, per the merge-and-ticket decision. (a) `echo` bypasses BOTH credential interpolation and masking: `echo "$TOKEN"` emits the literal `$TOKEN` here where Jenkins emits `****`, and an echo message already containing the value is never masked — the masker is wired to the shell path only. (b) A companion `X_FILE` can still overwrite an `X_FILE` bound by an OUTER `withCredentials`, because the collision check only sees the current call's names, not `ctx.EnvOverlay`. (c) The credential-argument regexes are unbounded, so `string(notcredentialsId: 'x', notvariable: 'T')` still binds — Jenkins rejects it, so the fail-closed gate is defeated by a name that merely ENDS with the key. (d) `stash` ignores Ant's default excludes (`.git/**` and friends), which Jenkins applies |
-| FG-002f | P1 | **DONE** | Audit every `isDiagnosticLine` pattern for user-reproducibility | The SAME defect has now been found three times: `Terminated` matched unconditionally, then the changelog warning by prefix, then a Groovy compile report by prefix — and the third was added *while fixing* the second. The shape is always identical: a pattern a user's own output can match gets suppressed from the compared output AND counted as a reported failure reason, so one engine failing silently can still read PROVEN. Remaining moderate-risk patterns to adjudicate: `FATAL:`, `Failed in branch `, `Aborted by `, `^at …(…)$`, `No artifacts found`, `\d+ of \d+ test(s) failed`. **Discharged empirically, by deleting each candidate and re-running all 49 receipts.** Result: **six of the suppressions were DEAD** — `FATAL:`, `No artifacts found`, `N of M test(s) failed`, `Aborted by `, the compile-report trio, and the skipped-stage regex — every one a latent false-PROVEN path protecting nothing. Two were load-bearing and are now handled without a user-reproducible match: Fogell **emits** `Failed in branch <name>` as Jenkins does (so neither copy needs suppressing), and Java stack frames are **context-gated** to the window opened by an exception line. The finding underneath: my instinct on a divergence was to add an exclusion rather than check whether the engines already agree — six times out of eight, they did |
